1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,360
Four questions a CTO can ask about one pull
request, a proposal on GitHub to merge

2
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:14,200
one branch into another. "The tests passed
on your laptop and failed in CI on the

3
00:00:14,240 --> 00:00:19,100
same commit. Was it the same commit?" "The
build stamped the release with a commit

4
00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:22,028
ID and not with a version.
Who removed the tags?"

5
00:00:22,368 --> 00:00:25,817
"This workflow has a paths
filter and is a required check.

6
00:00:25,857 --> 00:00:29,426
Why is the pull request waiting
forever?" "The job is green.

7
00:00:29,466 --> 00:00:32,675
Did the tests run, or did a
pipe swallow the failure?"

8
00:00:32,715 --> 00:00:36,103
CI, by the way, is the automated
checking of each change.

9
00:00:36,443 --> 00:00:41,118
The chapter makes a claim about these four,
and it's the claim of this whole part of

10
00:00:41,158 --> 00:00:44,373
the course: none of them is
answered by knowing YAML keys.

11
00:00:44,413 --> 00:00:48,919
Each is answered by knowing what a runner,
the machine that runs your job, has on

12
00:00:48,959 --> 00:00:51,894
disk and in its environment
when your command starts.

13
00:00:52,234 --> 00:00:57,650
Which commit, how much history, which
shell with which flags, which token, which

14
00:00:57,690 --> 00:01:03,175
variables. And one smaller puzzle for
today. A team asks for Python 3.10 and gets

15
00:01:03,215 --> 00:01:07,130
Python 3.1, with no error
anywhere. Keep that one in mind.

16
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:08,699
You'll watch it happen.

17
00:01:10,539 --> 00:01:15,688
Welcome back to Git and GitHub Deep Mastery.
Pull up a chair, and welcome to Part 6.

18
00:01:15,728 --> 00:01:19,888
So far the course has been about
Git and about GitHub as a platform.

19
00:01:19,928 --> 00:01:24,768
From here on there's a third layer, and
you'll see its lower-third label often.

20
00:01:25,108 --> 00:01:30,071
The textbook's framing: everything in this
part except the repository content is

21
00:01:30,111 --> 00:01:35,074
GitHub Actions, a service that reacts to
events on GitHub by running commands on

22
00:01:35,114 --> 00:01:40,014
machines. Git appears in exactly one
place, when a job fetches commits onto the

23
00:01:40,054 --> 00:01:45,017
runner. That place causes most of the
surprises, and it has its own video, video

24
00:01:45,057 --> 00:01:45,268
145.

25
00:01:45,608 --> 00:01:50,359
This video does two things. It gives you
the model: seven terms and which contains

26
00:01:50,399 --> 00:01:55,267
which. And it teaches you to read YAML, the
indented text format that workflow files

27
00:01:55,307 --> 00:01:59,941
are written in, as data, because a workflow
file is parsed before GitHub Actions

28
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:01,344
interprets a single key.

29
00:02:01,684 --> 00:02:05,214
One statement about evidence,
which holds for all of Part 6.

30
00:02:05,254 --> 00:02:08,726
Nothing in these chapters was
run on GitHub by the authors.

31
00:02:08,766 --> 00:02:13,546
The workflow files of the course were
parse-checked and assembled from documented

32
00:02:13,586 --> 00:02:17,533
syntax and from each action's own
definition at the pinned version.

33
00:02:17,573 --> 00:02:21,937
They weren't executed on GitHub by the
author. The labs have you run them.

34
00:02:21,977 --> 00:02:26,936
So when a workflow fails for a reason the
authors couldn't test, that's material for

35
00:02:26,976 --> 00:02:29,376
diagnosis and not a defect in your setup.

36
00:02:31,216 --> 00:02:32,794
Here's what you'll walk away with.

37
00:02:33,134 --> 00:02:34,546
After this video you can:

38
00:02:34,886 --> 00:02:39,849
define workflow, event, job, step, action,
runner and shell and say which contains

39
00:02:39,889 --> 00:02:40,216
which;

40
00:02:40,556 --> 00:02:44,947
read a workflow file in a fixed order to
predict when it runs, where, and with what;

41
00:02:45,287 --> 00:02:49,197
name the YAML features that change the
meaning of a workflow without an error;

42
00:02:49,537 --> 00:02:52,528
explain why the key on needs
care and what a tab does;

43
00:02:52,868 --> 00:02:56,713
say what was and was not verified
about the course's workflow files.

44
00:02:58,553 --> 00:03:03,401
In one sentence. A workflow is a YAML file
in the repository that says "when this

45
00:03:03,441 --> 00:03:08,470
event happens, run these jobs". Each job is
a list of steps that run in order on one

46
00:03:08,510 --> 00:03:13,478
fresh machine called a runner. A step is
either a shell command or an action, which

47
00:03:13,518 --> 00:03:14,806
is a packaged program.

48
00:03:15,146 --> 00:03:19,266
Precisely. The terms, as the
workflow syntax reference uses them.

49
00:03:19,606 --> 00:03:24,522
A workflow is a configurable automated
process defined by a YAML file.

50
00:03:24,562 --> 00:03:28,983
The file must be in .github/workflows
and end in .yml or .yaml.

51
00:03:29,323 --> 00:03:34,490
An event is an activity on GitHub, such
as a push, a pull request activity or a

52
00:03:34,530 --> 00:03:37,851
release. Or a schedule. Or
a manual or API request.

53
00:03:37,891 --> 00:03:40,751
An event isn't written
anywhere. It happens.

54
00:03:41,091 --> 00:03:45,569
The trigger is the on key of a workflow:
which events, with which filters, start a

55
00:03:45,609 --> 00:03:45,789
run.

56
00:03:46,129 --> 00:03:49,925
A workflow run is one execution
of a workflow for one event.

57
00:03:49,965 --> 00:03:54,145
It has a run ID, a run number and,
when re-run, an attempt number.

58
00:03:54,485 --> 00:03:58,119
A job is a set of steps that
execute on the same runner.

59
00:03:58,159 --> 00:04:02,120
Jobs of one run execute in
parallel unless needs orders them.

60
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:07,408
A step is one run command line or script,
or one uses reference to an action.

61
00:04:07,448 --> 00:04:09,481
Steps of a job execute in order.

62
00:04:09,821 --> 00:04:15,169
An action is a reusable program referenced
by uses, with an owner, a repository and

63
00:04:15,209 --> 00:04:20,233
a ref. It's a JavaScript program, a
composite of steps, or a Docker container,

64
00:04:20,273 --> 00:04:23,154
declared by the action.yml
in its repository.

65
00:04:23,494 --> 00:04:28,372
A runner is the machine that executes
one job. A GitHub-hosted runner is a new

66
00:04:28,412 --> 00:04:31,083
virtual machine per
job, chosen by runs-on.

67
00:04:31,423 --> 00:04:34,821
And the shell is the program
that executes a run step.

68
00:04:34,861 --> 00:04:36,794
Each run step is a new process.

69
00:04:37,134 --> 00:04:40,816
Three properties follow from those
definitions, and they explain most of what the

70
00:04:40,856 --> 00:04:41,597
next videos show.

71
00:04:41,937 --> 00:04:46,821
First: a job is the unit of isolation.
A job starts on a fresh machine.

72
00:04:46,861 --> 00:04:52,162
Files, installed tools and environment
variables don't carry from job to job.

73
00:04:52,202 --> 00:04:56,115
Data crosses jobs only as job
outputs, which are strings.

74
00:04:56,155 --> 00:05:01,942
As artifacts, which are files. Or as caches,
which are files on a best-effort basis.

75
00:05:02,282 --> 00:05:07,412
Quick quiz. Step one of a job
runs cd src. Step two runs ls.

76
00:05:07,452 --> 00:05:13,702
Where does step two start? A, inside
src. B, back where step one started.

77
00:05:13,742 --> 00:05:14,736
Your answer?

78
00:05:19,576 --> 00:05:24,984
B. Second: a step is the unit of process.
Steps of one job share the filesystem of

79
00:05:25,024 --> 00:05:28,505
the runner, but each run
step is a new shell process.

80
00:05:28,545 --> 00:05:31,827
A cd or an export in one
step is gone in the next.

81
00:05:32,167 --> 00:05:35,075
Third: the workflow file is
versioned with the code.

82
00:05:35,115 --> 00:05:39,780
For push and pull_request the workflow
definition is read from the commit the event

83
00:05:39,820 --> 00:05:44,372
refers to, so a branch can change its own
CI. For several other events it is read

84
00:05:44,412 --> 00:05:46,016
from the default branch only.

85
00:05:46,356 --> 00:05:52,104
Inside .git. A workflow is an ordinary blob,
the object that holds the bytes of one

86
00:05:52,144 --> 00:05:56,288
file, at a path under
.github/workflows/ in a commit's tree.

87
00:05:56,328 --> 00:06:00,472
Nothing else in the repository's
Git data describes Actions.

88
00:06:00,512 --> 00:06:05,911
Runs, jobs, logs, artifacts, caches,
secrets and variables are GitHub objects.

89
00:06:05,951 --> 00:06:09,955
A clone doesn't contain them,
and git log can't show them.

90
00:06:10,295 --> 00:06:14,376
YAML. In one sentence: a workflow
file is data, not a script.

91
00:06:14,416 --> 00:06:19,780
A YAML parser turns it into nested maps,
lists and scalars before GitHub Actions

92
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:24,576
interprets any key, so indentation
and quoting decide what GitHub sees.

93
00:06:24,916 --> 00:06:28,176
Four rules cover the mistakes
that occur in workflow files.

94
00:06:28,516 --> 00:06:32,263
One. Indentation is structure,
and tabs aren't indentation.

95
00:06:32,303 --> 00:06:37,591
A key belongs to the map whose column it
sits in. A tab character in indentation is

96
00:06:37,631 --> 00:06:38,554
a syntax error.

97
00:06:38,894 --> 00:06:44,549
Two. An unquoted scalar is typed by its
shape. 3.10 without quotes is a number, and

98
00:06:44,589 --> 00:06:48,185
the number is 3.1. v1.0 is
a string because of the v.

99
00:06:48,225 --> 00:06:52,027
So quote everything that's a
version, a label or a glob.

100
00:06:52,067 --> 00:06:57,516
The filter pattern cheat sheet adds that
patterns starting with a star, a square

101
00:06:57,556 --> 00:07:03,005
bracket or an exclamation mark must be
quoted, because those characters are YAML

102
00:07:03,045 --> 00:07:03,486
syntax.

103
00:07:03,826 --> 00:07:09,096
Three. The vertical bar keeps line breaks.
The greater-than sign folds them into

104
00:07:09,136 --> 00:07:12,150
spaces. A multi-line run
script needs the bar.

105
00:07:12,490 --> 00:07:18,338
Four. An ampersand with a name marks a
node, and a star with the name reuses it.

106
00:07:18,378 --> 00:07:24,520
Anchors and aliases are standard YAML and
have been accepted in workflow files since

107
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:26,949
the eighteenth of September 2025.

108
00:07:28,789 --> 00:07:33,606
Analogy. A workflow is a standing order
at a print shop: "Whenever a manuscript

109
00:07:33,646 --> 00:07:38,772
arrives, give one copy to the proofreader
and one to the typesetter, each at a clean

110
00:07:38,812 --> 00:07:43,753
desk, each following a numbered checklist,
some items of which say 'use the house

111
00:07:43,793 --> 00:07:47,258
stamping machine'." The
manuscript arriving is the event.

112
00:07:47,298 --> 00:07:50,886
The two workers are two jobs.
The clean desk is the runner.

113
00:07:50,926 --> 00:07:54,698
The checklist is the steps. The
stamping machine is an action.

114
00:07:55,038 --> 00:07:58,067
The textbook says the analogy
breaks in two places.

115
00:07:58,107 --> 00:08:03,121
The desks are destroyed after each job, so
nothing left on one is found by the next.

116
00:08:03,161 --> 00:08:07,814
And the manuscript isn't handed over:
the worker must fetch it, and by default

117
00:08:07,854 --> 00:08:09,439
fetches only the last page.

118
00:08:09,779 --> 00:08:14,764
Hold on to "only the last page". It's the
shallow checkout, and it answers the CTO's

119
00:08:14,804 --> 00:08:15,721
second question.

120
00:08:16,061 --> 00:08:21,078
A reading order. The textbook explains
workflow 1 from top to bottom: name, trigger,

121
00:08:21,118 --> 00:08:26,075
permissions, job and runner, then the steps.
This course uses that as a fixed order

122
00:08:26,115 --> 00:08:29,928
for every workflow file you meet,
and you'll use it in the gate.

123
00:08:30,268 --> 00:08:33,348
First, the trigger. Which
events, with which filters?

124
00:08:33,388 --> 00:08:37,469
That tells you when a run exists at
all, and for which commit and ref.

125
00:08:37,809 --> 00:08:42,591
Second, the permissions. What may the job's
token, the credential it uses to call

126
00:08:42,631 --> 00:08:43,246
GitHub, do?

127
00:08:43,586 --> 00:08:47,722
Third, each job: on which runner, after
which other jobs, under which conditions.

128
00:08:48,062 --> 00:08:52,412
Fourth, the first steps of the job:
what's checked out, and how much of it.

129
00:08:52,752 --> 00:08:55,277
Fifth and last, the commands
that are the real check.

130
00:08:55,617 --> 00:08:59,977
People read in the opposite order. They
go to the test command first because it's

131
00:09:00,017 --> 00:09:04,431
familiar. But a correct test command that
runs on the wrong commit, or never runs,

132
00:09:04,471 --> 00:09:05,246
proves nothing.

133
00:09:07,086 --> 00:09:10,434
Read it from the outside
in. An event starts a run.

134
00:09:10,474 --> 00:09:13,890
The run holds two jobs, each
on its own new machine.

135
00:09:13,930 --> 00:09:17,677
Inside a job, every step is
an action or a shell process.

136
00:09:17,717 --> 00:09:22,727
And between build and report, only job
outputs cross, and those are strings.

137
00:09:24,567 --> 00:09:29,049
One caveat before the first output,
and the textbook is exact about it.

138
00:09:29,089 --> 00:09:34,017
The parser here is PyYAML, the library
that parse-checks the course workflows.

139
00:09:34,057 --> 00:09:37,265
It implements YAML 1.1.
GitHub uses its own parser.

140
00:09:37,305 --> 00:09:40,450
So read each result with
the note that follows it.

141
00:09:40,790 --> 00:09:42,316
Step 1: scalars.

142
00:09:42,656 --> 00:09:50,085
The file has a list of Python versions:
3.9, then 3.10 without quotes, then 3.10 in

143
00:09:50,125 --> 00:09:55,485
quotes, then 3.11. And three
more values: yes, NO, and v1.0.

144
00:09:55,525 --> 00:10:00,164
Predict the type and value
of each. Say it out loud.

145
00:10:05,004 --> 00:10:09,526
The second element is 3.1. There's
the puzzle from the opening.

146
00:10:09,566 --> 00:10:15,607
A matrix, a list of variants that each get
their own job, written with unquoted 3.10

147
00:10:15,647 --> 00:10:20,675
asks for Python 3.1. This is a property
of numbers, not of one parser.

148
00:10:20,715 --> 00:10:26,540
The quoted one stayed a string. yes and
NO became booleans, because YAML 1.1 says

149
00:10:26,580 --> 00:10:26,757
so.

150
00:10:27,097 --> 00:10:32,432
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper.
Rewrite that list of Python versions so that

151
00:10:32,472 --> 00:10:35,119
every element arrives as text. I'll wait.

152
00:10:39,959 --> 00:10:45,195
Put each version in quotes. Quoted, 3.10
keeps its zero, exactly as the third

153
00:10:45,235 --> 00:10:50,883
element did. That's rule two: quote everything
that's a version, a label or a glob.

154
00:10:51,223 --> 00:10:56,246
One point is unverified. Whether GitHub's
workflow parser also reads unquoted yes,

155
00:10:56,286 --> 00:11:01,309
no, on and off values as booleans isn't
stated on the documentation pages read for

156
00:11:01,349 --> 00:11:05,138
the chapter. Quote such values
and the question doesn't arise.

157
00:11:05,478 --> 00:11:07,033
Step 2: the key on.

158
00:11:07,373 --> 00:11:12,164
A minimal workflow with name, on and jobs.
What will the three top-level keys be

159
00:11:12,204 --> 00:11:14,338
after parsing? Make your prediction.

160
00:11:19,178 --> 00:11:23,186
name, true, and jobs. The key on
came out as the boolean true.

161
00:11:23,226 --> 00:11:27,625
GitHub Actions documents on as the
trigger key and reads it as such.

162
00:11:27,665 --> 00:11:32,978
A generic YAML 1.1 tool doesn't. The
consequence is for your own tooling: a script

163
00:11:33,018 --> 00:11:37,156
that loads workflow files with
PyYAML must look up the key True.

164
00:11:37,196 --> 00:11:39,441
The course's own checker does that.

165
00:11:39,781 --> 00:11:44,458
Also notice pull_request: null. An event
with no configuration is a key with an

166
00:11:44,498 --> 00:11:49,414
empty value, and the documentation requires
the colon on every event once any event

167
00:11:49,454 --> 00:11:51,146
in the map has configuration.

168
00:11:51,486 --> 00:11:52,955
Step 3: anchors.

169
00:11:59,295 --> 00:12:03,157
The test job defines two anchors.
The lint job reuses them.

170
00:12:03,197 --> 00:12:06,994
After parsing, the alias is
indistinguishable from a copy.

171
00:12:07,034 --> 00:12:11,293
So an anchor removes repetition
inside one file and nothing more.

172
00:12:11,333 --> 00:12:12,682
It can't cross files.

173
00:12:13,022 --> 00:12:17,631
One more point is unverified. YAML merge
keys aren't mentioned by the anchors

174
00:12:17,671 --> 00:12:22,703
documentation or the changelog entry. The
course's research notes mark their support

175
00:12:22,743 --> 00:12:26,085
as unverified, and the course's
examples don't use them.

176
00:12:26,425 --> 00:12:28,319
Step 4: block scalars.

177
00:12:28,659 --> 00:12:33,767
The same two command lines, once under a
vertical bar and once under a greater-than

178
00:12:33,807 --> 00:12:37,055
sign. What string does each
produce? Say it out loud.

179
00:12:41,895 --> 00:12:47,452
With the bar: two lines. With the greater-than
sign: one line, uv sync --locked uv

180
00:12:47,492 --> 00:12:51,548
run pytest. That's one wrong
command and not two right ones.

181
00:12:51,588 --> 00:12:55,166
The file is valid YAML both
ways. No error tells you.

182
00:12:55,506 --> 00:12:57,117
Step 5: a tab.

183
00:12:57,457 --> 00:13:01,725
This is the one mistake of the five that's
reported: a scanner error with the line

184
00:13:01,765 --> 00:13:02,513
and the column.

185
00:13:02,853 --> 00:13:05,227
Now read workflow 1 in
the order you learned.

186
00:13:05,567 --> 00:13:08,771
The trigger: pushes to main,
and pull request activity.

187
00:13:08,811 --> 00:13:13,312
The branch filter on push stops a second
run for every push to a pull request

188
00:13:13,352 --> 00:13:15,790
branch, which pull_request already covers.

189
00:13:16,130 --> 00:13:20,844
The permissions: contents: read. The job
token may read repository contents, and

190
00:13:20,884 --> 00:13:25,421
every other permission is none. That's
least privilege, and it makes the file

191
00:13:25,461 --> 00:13:28,274
independent of the
repository's default setting.

192
00:13:28,614 --> 00:13:34,620
The job: one job with the ID test and the
display name "Unit tests", on the fixed

193
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:37,606
image ubuntu-24.04, at most ten minutes.

194
00:13:37,946 --> 00:13:43,287
The checkout: an action, pinned to a full
commit ID with the version as a comment.

195
00:13:43,327 --> 00:13:48,471
Read the ID from the file and from
workflows/ACTION_PINS.md, never from memory.

196
00:13:48,511 --> 00:13:52,606
persist-credentials: false means:
don't keep the token on disk.

197
00:13:52,946 --> 00:13:56,785
Then a shell step that prints the event,
the ref, the commit, whether the clone is

198
00:13:56,825 --> 00:14:00,663
shallow, and the branch status. The textbook
calls this the first thing to read in

199
00:14:00,703 --> 00:14:02,082
any log: what was checked out.

200
00:14:02,422 --> 00:14:06,858
Then Python 3.13, with the version
quoted, as you now know why.

201
00:14:06,898 --> 00:14:12,682
And last, the check itself: the unit tests.
A non-zero exit status fails the step,

202
00:14:12,722 --> 00:14:14,103
the job and the run.

203
00:14:14,443 --> 00:14:18,474
Lab 26.1 on your own practice
repository, in your normal shell.

204
00:14:18,514 --> 00:14:23,837
The interface changes. The lab text and the
linked documentation are the reference.

205
00:14:23,877 --> 00:14:26,745
No GitHub output was
captured by the authors.

206
00:14:27,085 --> 00:14:31,601
You copy the sample project, add workflow
1, commit, and create the repository with

207
00:14:31,641 --> 00:14:36,212
gh repo create and --push. Section 15.22
labels that command 🟡 CAUTION: it creates a

208
00:14:36,252 --> 00:14:40,714
repository on GitHub, a remote and a push,
and what reaches a public repository is

209
00:14:40,754 --> 00:14:44,940
published. Before you run it, the lab
makes you predict: which event the push

210
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:49,496
causes, which workflow starts, what GITHUB_REF
and GITHUB_SHA will be, and how many

211
00:14:49,536 --> 00:14:51,088
commits the runner will have.

212
00:14:51,428 --> 00:14:56,267
Both read. In the browser, open the Actions
tab, find the run, open the job, and

213
00:14:56,307 --> 00:15:00,902
expand the step "Show what was checked
out". Compare its six lines with your

214
00:15:00,942 --> 00:15:01,573
prediction.

215
00:15:03,413 --> 00:15:05,061
Five mistakes to watch for.

216
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:11,379
An unquoted version such as 3.10 in a
matrix. Root cause: an unquoted scalar is

217
00:15:11,419 --> 00:15:14,883
typed by its shape, and
as a number it is 3.1.

218
00:15:15,223 --> 00:15:20,420
A multi-line script under >. Root cause:
the folded form joins the lines with

219
00:15:20,460 --> 00:15:24,977
spaces, so two commands become one
wrong command, without an error.

220
00:15:25,317 --> 00:15:30,287
A custom tool that looks for the key on
after loading the file with a YAML 1.1

221
00:15:30,327 --> 00:15:34,205
parser. Root cause: that parser
reads on as the boolean true.

222
00:15:34,545 --> 00:15:38,578
Expecting a file or an installed
tool from one job in the next.

223
00:15:38,618 --> 00:15:43,168
Root cause: a job is the unit of
isolation and runs on a fresh machine.

224
00:15:43,508 --> 00:15:46,596
Reading the test command
first and the trigger last.

225
00:15:46,636 --> 00:15:51,527
Root cause: whether a run exists, and for
which commit, is decided before any step

226
00:15:51,567 --> 00:15:51,828
runs.

227
00:15:53,668 --> 00:15:59,362
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation team adds
Python 3.10 to the test matrix of their

228
00:15:59,402 --> 00:16:05,096
harness, so that the nightly run covers the
interpreter their largest customer still

229
00:16:05,136 --> 00:16:09,192
uses. The pull request is one
line: a new element in a list.

230
00:16:09,232 --> 00:16:14,244
It's reviewed in a minute. The new
matrix job doesn't ask for Python 3.10.

231
00:16:14,284 --> 00:16:15,814
It asks for Python 3.1.

232
00:16:16,154 --> 00:16:21,490
The reviewer saw 3.10. The parser saw
3.1. The repair is two quote characters.

233
00:16:21,530 --> 00:16:27,210
The prevention is the rule from this video,
applied in review: every version, label

234
00:16:27,250 --> 00:16:32,930
and glob in a workflow file is quoted, and
a reviewer who sees an unquoted one asks

235
00:16:32,970 --> 00:16:34,101
what type it has.

236
00:16:35,941 --> 00:16:40,431
Your turn. Do Lab 26.1,
"Workflow 1, run the tests", in

237
00:16:40,471 --> 00:16:46,772
lab-manual/m26-actions-fundamentals.md,
in your normal shell on your practice

238
00:16:46,812 --> 00:16:47,842
organization.

239
00:16:48,182 --> 00:16:52,338
The lab's second step is the prediction,
and it's the point of the lab.

240
00:16:52,378 --> 00:16:57,007
Write down, before you push: the event,
the workflow that starts, the values of

241
00:16:57,047 --> 00:17:00,967
GITHUB_REF and GITHUB_SHA, and the
number of commits on the runner.

242
00:17:01,007 --> 00:17:02,031
Then read the log.

243
00:17:02,371 --> 00:17:08,892
The challenge is Exercise 26.3, "Six
things the author did not mean", in

244
00:17:08,932 --> 00:17:12,355
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

245
00:17:16,195 --> 00:17:20,768
Answer out loud first. A strong answer gives
an order and a reason for each position

246
00:17:20,808 --> 00:17:25,216
in it, in the terms of the model: event,
ref and commit, token, runner, checkout,

247
00:17:25,256 --> 00:17:28,016
commands. It says what each
stage lets you predict.

248
00:17:28,056 --> 00:17:32,629
The follow-up asks what you still can't know
from the repository alone after reading

249
00:17:32,669 --> 00:17:37,242
all twelve. Think about which of the things
in this video are GitHub objects and not

250
00:17:37,282 --> 00:17:37,736
Git data.

251
00:17:39,576 --> 00:17:44,471
Let's land this. One event, one run, one
job on a fresh runner, steps in order, and

252
00:17:44,511 --> 00:17:48,573
a result at the end. And before any
of that, a parser reads the file.

253
00:17:48,913 --> 00:17:50,573
You should now be able to say:

254
00:17:50,913 --> 00:17:56,107
An event starts a run of a workflow; a run
has jobs; each job runs on its own fresh

255
00:17:56,147 --> 00:18:00,144
runner; a job has steps; a step
is an action or a shell process.

256
00:18:00,484 --> 00:18:04,457
A job is the unit of isolation and
a step is the unit of process.

257
00:18:04,797 --> 00:18:09,740
A workflow file is an ordinary blob in a
commit; runs, logs, secrets and variables

258
00:18:09,780 --> 00:18:12,293
are GitHub objects that no clone contains.

259
00:18:12,633 --> 00:18:17,686
YAML is parsed before Actions reads it:
quote versions and globs, use the bar for

260
00:18:17,726 --> 00:18:19,007
scripts, never a tab.

261
00:18:19,347 --> 00:18:24,103
The course's workflow files were parse-checked
and assembled from documentation, and

262
00:18:24,143 --> 00:18:25,929
not run on GitHub by the author.

263
00:18:27,769 --> 00:18:36,252
Read sections 20A.1 to 20A.3 of Chapter
20A. Do Exercise 26.1, "True or false, with

264
00:18:36,292 --> 00:18:41,796
the reason", in
exercises/m26-m31-actions-security.md.

265
00:18:42,136 --> 00:18:47,321
Today you learned the seven terms of the
Actions model, and you caught YAML changing

266
00:18:47,361 --> 00:18:52,422
a value without an error. Before the next
video, read workflow 1 once in the fixed

267
00:18:52,462 --> 00:18:55,719
order: trigger, permissions,
job, checkout, commands.

268
00:18:55,759 --> 00:19:00,882
